§§ `Chapter 37 -- wardship by reason of a lesser tenure`

-- 37.1 bars the King's wardship of the heir, or of the land held of the other lord, by reason of a
-- fee-farm, socage or burgage when the holder also holds of another lord by knight's service.
-- 37.2 is read as speaking of the same holder ("that fee-farm"), so it too needs the second
-- holding (fork F37).  37.3 names no second holding as a condition for the petty serjeanty
-- and is read as it stands.
-- src:81 | If anyone holds of us by fee-farm, by socage, or by burgage, and holds also land of another lord by knight’s service, we will not (by reason of that fee-farm, socage, or burgage,) have the wardship of the heir, or of such land of his as is of the fief of that other;
-- src:81 | nor shall we have wardship of that fee-farm, socage, or burgage, unless such fee-farm owes knight’s service.
-- src:81 | We will not by reason of any petty serjeanty which anyone may hold of us by the service of rendering to us knives, arrows, or the like, have wardship of his heir or of the land which he holds of another lord by knight’s service.
@ref Chapter 37 (src:81)
GIVEN tenure IS A TenureOfKing
      thing IS A WardshipOf
      `he holds also land of another lord by knight's service` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the fee-farm, socage or burgage owes knight's service` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Bar
DECIDE `ch 37 -- the King may have wardship of this by reason of the tenure` IS
    IF   tenure EQUALS `fee-farm`
      OR tenure EQUALS `socage`
      OR tenure EQUALS `burgage`
    THEN IF thing EQUALS `the fee-farm, socage or burgage itself`
         THEN IF `he holds also land of another lord by knight's service`
              THEN IF `the fee-farm, socage or burgage owes knight's service`
                   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless such fee-farm owes knight's service".
                   THEN `Not barred`
                   ELSE Barred
              -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If anyone holds of us by fee-farm, by socage, or by burgage, and holds also land of another lord by knight's service".
              ELSE `Not barred`
         ELSE IF `he holds also land of another lord by knight's service`
              THEN Barred
              -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If anyone holds of us by fee-farm, by socage, or by burgage, and holds also land of another lord by knight's service".
              ELSE `Not barred`
    ELSE IF tenure EQUALS `petty serjeanty held of the King by rendering knives, arrows or the like`
         THEN IF thing EQUALS `the fee-farm, socage or burgage itself`
              -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: if, unless because "if" and "unless" govern fee-farm, socage and burgage; this branch is reached by a petty serjeanty and the fee-farm itself, which 37.3 does not mention).
              THEN `Bar not covered`
              ELSE Barred
         -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: if, unless because "if" and "unless" govern fee-farm, socage and burgage; the branch is reached by a tenure the text does not name).
         ELSE `Bar not covered`

-- src:81 | If anyone holds of us by fee-farm, by socage, or by burgage, and holds also land of another lord by knight’s service, we will not (by reason of that fee-farm, socage, or burgage,) have the wardship of the heir, or of such land of his as is of the fief of that other;
-- src:81 | nor shall we have wardship of that fee-farm, socage, or burgage, unless such fee-farm owes knight’s service.
-- src:81 | We will not by reason of any petty serjeanty which anyone may hold of us by the service of rendering to us knives, arrows, or the like, have wardship of his heir or of the land which he holds of another lord by knight’s service.
@ref Chapter 37 (src:81)
GIVEN tenure IS A TenureOfKing
      thing IS A WardshipOf
