§§ `Chapter 38 -- putting a man to his law`

-- gloss (not the text's): "put to his law" is the text's phrase for requiring a man to clear himself
-- on oath; "credible witnesses" is taken as a plain fact.  Only a bailiff is named.
-- src:83 | No bailiff for the future shall put any man to his “law” upon his own mere word of mouth, without credible witnesses brought for this purpose.
@ref Chapter 38 (src:83)
GIVEN officer IS A Party
      `credible witnesses are brought for this purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Bar
DECIDE `ch 38 -- the officer may put a man to his law upon his own mere word of mouth` IS
    IF officer EQUALS `a bailiff`
    THEN IF `credible witnesses are brought for this purpose`
         -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "without credible witnesses brought for this purpose" describes the barred act; it does not say that a man put to his law with witnesses is allowed (F64c).
         THEN `Bar not covered`
         ELSE Barred
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text does not speak to a case outside the facts this rule names.
    ELSE `Bar not covered`

-- src:83 | No bailiff for the future shall put any man to his “law” upon his own mere word of mouth, without credible witnesses brought for this purpose.
@ref Chapter 38 (src:83)
GIVEN officer IS A Party
      `credible witnesses are brought for this purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
DECIDE `ch 38 -- no bailiff shall put any man to his law upon his own mere word of mouth, without credible witnesses` IS
    IF `ch 38 -- the officer may put a man to his law upon his own mere word of mouth` officer `credible witnesses are brought for this purpose` EQUALS Barred
    THEN PARTY officer
         SHANT `put a man to his law upon his own mere word of mouth, without credible witnesses`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BY officer BECAUSE "ch 38: a man was put to his law upon the mere word of mouth of a bailiff, without credible witnesses"
    ELSE FULFILLED
